---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';

// Linked from the Android app's About screen, its Google Play listing and the
// Chrome Web Store listing, which all require a public privacy policy URL. Keep
// it in step with mobile/PLAY_STORE.md (Data safety answers) and
// extensions/browser/STORE_LISTING.md (Privacy practices) whenever data
// handling changes.
const updated = 'November 22, 2026';
// Same date, ISO form — parsing the string above shifts it a day in some time zones.
const updatedIso = '2026-11-22';

// The SEO audit in CI requires structured data on every indexable page.
const site = Astro.site ?? Astro.url;
const privacySchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Privacy policy',
  description:
    'What Cohesive Colors stores, where it lives and how to remove it, for the website, the Android app and the browser extension.',
  url: new URL('/privacy', site).href,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  dateModified: updatedIso,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Cohesive Colors', url: new URL('/', site).href },
};
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What Cohesive Colors stores, where it lives and how to remove it, for the website, the Android app and the browser extension."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-ink-700">
    <h1 class="text-[28px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-900">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[12.5px] text-ink-500">
      Last updated {updated}. Applies to cohesive-colors.github.io, the Cohesive Colors Android app and the
      Cohesive Colors browser extension.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink-900">The short version</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      There are no accounts, no ads, no analytics and no tracking. Your saved palettes never leave your device.
      The only things sent to a server are likes and the palettes you choose to publish, tied to a random
      anonymous ID rather than to you.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink-900">What stays on your device</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Saved (bookmarked) palettes, the cohesion overlay settings and, when the shared feed is unavailable, your
      likes. On the website they live in your browser's local storage; in the app they live in the app's private
      storage. Clearing site data, using "Clear saved palettes" in the app, or uninstalling the app removes them.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink-900">What is sent to a server</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Likes and published palettes are stored in Google Firebase Realtime Database so everyone sees the same
      counts and feed. To stop one person liking a palette many times, Firebase Authentication creates an
      anonymous ID for each browser or app install. It is not linked to your name, email, phone number or any
      other account. Stored with that ID:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
      <li>the IDs of palettes you liked;</li>
      <li>palettes you publish: their colours, generated name, harmony, settings and publish time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Published palettes are public by design and appear in the shared feed. Like any web service, Firebase
      processes technical data such as IP addresses to deliver requests; see
      <a class="underline" href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase's privacy information</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink-900">Browser extension</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      The extension sends nothing to any server. Its saved palettes and settings live in your browser's extension
      storage and are removed when you uninstall it. When you open the "From page" tab, it reads the colours used
      by the page in your current tab (computed styles only: no text, form data or browsing history) to build a
      palette, only for that tab and only at that moment. The result stays in the extension. It does not use the
      shared feed, likes or Firebase.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink-900">What we do not do</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      We do not sell or share data, show ads, use analytics or advertising SDKs, or request access to your
      contacts, location, camera, microphone or files.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink-900">Removing your data</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Un-liking a palette removes that like. Local data goes away as described above. Published palettes cannot be
      edited from the app; to have one removed, contact us through the developer email on the Google Play listing
      and include the palette's link.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink-900">Children</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">The service is a general-audience design tool and is not directed at children under 13.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink-900">Changes</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it.</p>
  </article>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(privacySchema)} />
</Layout>
